From: LUG-Wine-Experimental <lug-wine-experimental@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] winevulkan: Zero-initialize conversion_context allocations

When converting Vulkan struct chains (such as VkPhysicalDeviceProperties2 or
VkPhysicalDeviceFeatures2) from Windows to host in winevulkan thunks, output
extension structs marked returnedonly="true" in vk.xml (for example,
VkPhysicalDeviceLayeredDriverPropertiesMSFT) are allocated via
conversion_context_alloc() with only sType and pNext initialized.

Per the Vulkan specification, host drivers that do not implement an extension
in a pNext chain ignore the unrecognized structure and leave its fields
untouched. Because conversion_context_alloc() previously returned uninitialized
stack/heap memory, convert_*_host_to_win64/win32 subsequently copied uninitialized
garbage back over the caller's zero-initialized struct.

In Star Citizen 4.10+, CGPUPhysicalDeviceVulkan::GetPhysicalDeviceInfos() chains
a zero-initialized VkPhysicalDeviceLayeredDriverPropertiesMSFT struct to
vkGetPhysicalDeviceProperties2 and disqualifies any physical device whose
underlyingAPI != VK_LAYERED_DRIVER_UNDERLYING_API_NONE_MSFT (0), causing all
discrete GPUs to be rejected ("[VK] No valid Discrete GPU found") and crashing
with STATUS_CRYENGINE_FATAL_ERROR (0x2BADF00D).

Zero-initialize allocations in conversion_context_alloc() so unsupported pNext
output structs preserve zeroed fields instead of leaking stack/heap garbage.
---
 dlls/winevulkan/vulkan_private.h | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/dlls/winevulkan/vulkan_private.h b/dlls/winevulkan/vulkan_private.h
index 06932593b21..8f9d6042a1b 100644
--- a/dlls/winevulkan/vulkan_private.h
+++ b/dlls/winevulkan/vulkan_private.h
@@ -21,6 +21,7 @@
 #define __WINE_VULKAN_PRIVATE_H
 
 #include <pthread.h>
+#include <string.h>
 
 #include "vulkan_loader.h"
 #include "vulkan_thunks.h"
@@ -87,12 +88,13 @@ static inline void *conversion_context_alloc(struct conversion_context *pool, si
     {
         void *ret = pool->buffer + pool->used;
         pool->used += (size + sizeof(UINT64) - 1) & ~(sizeof(UINT64) - 1);
+        memset(ret, 0, size);
         return ret;
     }
     else
     {
         struct list *entry;
-        if (!(entry = malloc(sizeof(*entry) + size)))
+        if (!(entry = calloc(1, sizeof(*entry) + size)))
             return NULL;
         list_add_tail(&pool->alloc_entries, entry);
         return entry + 1;
